WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.830
Not every mountain requires an entire region of thickened crust.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.310
A local mountain can sit on the existing crust.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:10.930
Volcanic eruptions pile lava, ash, and scoria onto a surface, building

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:14.290
anything from a small cinder cone to a large composite volcano.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.290
An eroded volcanic remnant may remain high because its rocks resist

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.350
erosion, even after its original cone shape has disappeared.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.380
Reverse faulting can raise one block over another.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.500
The Front Range and its tilted neighboring layers illustrate that relationship.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.760
Normal faults can also produce mountains, despite extending and thinning the

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.180
region. One block drops into a basin while the neighboring block

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.260
remains relatively high or rises.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.920
Near Death Valley, mountains and sediment-trapping basins are paired consequences of

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:44.940
normal faulting.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:48.410
Folding bends both rock layers and the land above them.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.970
A buried thrust can push layers upward without breaking through the

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:56.010
surface. The Los Angeles example connects hidden structures with folded rock

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.450
and damaged wells. A fold can also survive as relief long

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:03.270
after deformation ends, if erosion strips away weak cover and leaves

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.050
a resistant folded layer exposed.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.360
Differential erosion creates mountains by removing surrounding rocks faster.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.440
Stone Mountain is granite that crystallized deep underground and was later

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.740
uncovered. It is not a volcano simply because it is an

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.200
igneous mountain. A hard cap can protect softer layers and leave

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.520
a flat-topped mesa. Resistant tilted beds can produce ridges whose shapes

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.580
follow the rock layers.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.190
So ask what made the relief:

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:33.190
material added at the surface, displacement on a fault, bending of

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:37.170
layers, or unequal erosion? More than one process can contribute.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.290
A mountain's outline alone does not identify its origin.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.810
Use the rock types, layer geometry, fault relationships, and surrounding landscape

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.310
to distinguish the mechanisms.
